# Canaries for `--phase effects`, the declared-vs-inferred effect report.
#
# This file is not a behaviour test: it is the instrument's own calibration
# rig. Every function below has a KNOWN correct answer, written next to it, so
# that a report which silently says nothing is caught as a failure rather than
# read as "the codebase is clean". An earlier hand-rolled version of this
# instrument reported zero gaps across 316 declarations and was believed for a
# while; it was only caught by checking it against a case known to be broken.
#
# No module header on purpose: the prelude is then not bundled into the IR, but
# it IS still loaded into the typecheck env (that is why `print` resolves here),
# so the report stays a handful of lines instead of hundreds.

# --- Must be flagged: declared pure, body reaches IO directly. ---
# This is the case docs/spec-v0.md §7's enforcement note names as accepted today.
fn shout(s: String) -> Unit = print(s)

# --- Must NOT be flagged: declared !{IO}, body does IO. Honest. ---
fn loud(s: String) -> Unit !{IO} = print(s)

# --- Must NOT be flagged: over-declaring is allowed (inferred pure ⊑ declared IO).
# `mutvec_len` depends on exactly this: its body is a pure `vector_length` call
# and its signature is !{IO} because the length is mutable state.
fn over_declared(n: Int) -> Int !{IO} = n + 1

# --- Must NOT be flagged: pure declaration, pure body. The overwhelming majority.
fn honest_pure(n: Int) -> Int = n * 2

# --- Must be flagged: one hop through a correctly-annotated callee. ---
fn calls_loud(s: String) -> Unit = loud(s)

# --- Must NOT be flagged, and this is a LIMITATION, not a success. ---
# `shout` is declared pure, so its arrow says pure, so this caller looks clean.
# The report is one wave of propagation, not a fixed point: this function can
# only surface after `shout` above is annotated. The D1 migration number read
# off a single run is therefore a LOWER BOUND.
fn calls_shout(s: String) -> Unit = shout(s)

# --- Must be flagged: the effect enters through a lambda argument. ---
# Two independent mechanisms can flag this, and that is worth knowing when it
# breaks. The older one is `infer_lambda` reporting the lambda BODY's effect as
# the effect of the enclosing expression — an attribution that is technically
# wrong, since building a closure performs no IO. The newer one is real: the
# lambda's arrow carries !{IO}, unifying it against `list_each`'s `!{e}` binds
# e := IO. Once infer_lambda's attribution is corrected, only the second remains,
# and this line is what proves the second is actually doing the work.
fn each_lambda(xs: List String) -> Unit = list_each(\x -> print(x), xs)

# --- Must be flagged: the effect enters through a NAMED function argument. ---
# This was the known blind spot until effect unification landed. Passing `print`
# by name rather than wrapping it in a lambda routes around infer_lambda
# entirely: the reference is just a value. It is caught now only because
# `unify_applied`'s TFunc arm unifies the arrows' effect fields, binding
# `list_each`'s `!{e}` to IO. This is THE canary for that channel — if it stops
# flagging, effect unification has been disconnected, and `each_lambda` above
# will keep passing on the over-approximation alone and hide it.
fn each_named(xs: List String) -> Unit = list_each(print, xs)

# --- Must NOT be flagged: a pure callback through the same HOF. ---
# `list_each`'s `!{e}` must be FRESHENED at each instantiation. If it is not,
# every `!{e}` in the program shares one global EffectVar("e"), the binding
# e := IO from the two uses above leaks into this one, and a genuinely pure
# function reports a gap. That is a FALSE POSITIVE, the one failure direction
# the enforcement step cannot tolerate: at step 4 it becomes a compile error on
# correct code. Guards `scheme_effect_vars` actually being populated.
fn each_pure(xs: List String) -> Unit = list_each(\x -> (), xs)

# --- Must NOT be flagged: BUILDING a closure that does IO is pure. ---
# The `examples/http_web_server.routes` shape, reduced. Evaluating this allocates
# a closure and returns it; no output is written. The IO belongs to the returned
# ARROW (`String -> Unit !{IO}`), and fires when a caller invokes it.
#
# This was the last false positive standing between the report and enforcement:
# all six real corpus gaps were this shape, so flipping the report to an error
# would have rejected six correct programs. See docs/effect-enforcement-v0.md §9.4.
fn make_shouter() -> String -> Unit !{IO} = \s -> print(s)

# --- Must be flagged: the closure is built AND applied, right here. ---
# The counterweight to make_shouter, and it exercises a different code path:
# the callee is an expression, not a name, so it goes through infer_call_general,
# which merges the effect of EVALUATING the callee — not the arrow's effect.
# Dropping infer_lambda's over-approximation without also reading the arrow here
# makes this report pure, which is the quiet-failure direction.
fn apply_now(s: String) -> Unit = (\x -> print(x))(s)

# --- Must be flagged: closure bound to a local, then called. ---
# The third path: `f` is a variable, so the effect must come from its arrow via
# infer_call_var/arrows_effect. Between this, apply_now and each_named, all three
# ways an effect can reach a call site are pinned.
fn via_local(s: String) -> Unit =
  let f = \x -> print(x)
  in f(s)

# --- Must NOT be flagged: `panic` is PURE, deliberately. ---
# An abort has no continuation, so nothing downstream can observe it — see
# docs/effect-enforcement-v0.md §6 for the survey behind that call. Before the
# 2026-08-16 decision `panic` was `!{IO}`, which made every exhaustiveness arm of
# this shape report as an effect gap: nine of the twelve gaps in the first corpus
# census were this and nothing else. If this line ever reports GAP again,
# `panic`'s declaration has drifted back.
fn unreachable_arm(n: Int) -> Int =
  if n < 0 then panic("unreachable_arm: negative (internal error)") else n

# --- Instance methods are a SECOND declaration boundary, with its own discard. ---
# infer.sprout has two sites that bind a body's inferred effect; the `fn` one and
# the `instance` one. Instrumenting only the first would leave every instance
# method out of the census — silently, and in the direction that reads as good
# news. These two pin that both are recorded.
class Describe a {
  fn describe(x: a) -> String
}

type Quiet = Quiet
type Noisy = Noisy

# Must NOT be flagged: pure method, pure body.
instance Describe Quiet {
  fn describe(x: Quiet) -> String =
    "quiet"
}

# Must be flagged: pure method signature, body reaches IO.
instance Describe Noisy {
  fn describe(x: Noisy) -> String =
    do
      print("noisy")
      "noisy"
}

fn main() -> Unit !{IO} =
  do
    loud("calibration")
    honest_pure(1)
    ()
